  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "A coin is projected vertically down from a skyscraper with a height of $720\\,\\text{m}.$ It takes $12\\,\\text{s}$ to hit the ground. What was the initial velocity of the coin?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*",
  "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The coin falls for $t=12\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- The coin covers the distance of $s=720\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (720) & =u(12)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(12)^{2} \\\\ 720 & =12u+705.6 \\\\ 14.4 & =12u \\\\ u & =1.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the coin was $1.2\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
